⟦P0⟧Publishing a module⟦P1⟧When you want to make a module available for other developers, you publish it so⟦P2⟧that it's visible to Go tools. Once you've published the module, developers⟦P3⟧importing its packages will be able to resolve a dependency on the module by⟦P4⟧running commands such as ⟦P5⟧.⟦P6⟧⟦P7⟧Note:⟦P8⟧ Don't change a tagged version of a module after publishing it. For⟦P9⟧developers using the module, Go tools authenticate a downloaded module against⟦P10⟧the first downloaded copy. If the two differ, Go tools will return a security⟦P11⟧error. Instead of changing the code for a previously published version, publish⟦P12⟧a new version.⟦P13⟧⟦P14⟧See also⟦P15⟧⟦P16⟧For an overview of module development, see ⟦P17⟧Developing and publishing⟦P18⟧modules⟦P19⟧⟦P20⟧For a high-level module development workflow ⟦P21⟧ which includes publishing ⟦P22⟧⟦P23⟧see ⟦P24⟧Module release and versioning workflow⟦P25⟧.⟦P26⟧Publishing steps⟦P27⟧Use the following steps to publish a module.⟦P28⟧Open a command prompt and change to your module's root directory in the local⟦P29⟧repository.⟦P30⟧Run ⟦P31⟧, which removes any dependencies the module might have⟦P32⟧accumulated that are no longer necessary.⟦P33⟧Run ⟦P34⟧ a final time to make sure everything is working.⟦P35⟧This runs the unit tests you've written to use the Go testing framework.⟦P36⟧Tag the project with a new version number using the ⟦P37⟧ command.⟦P38⟧For the version number, use a number that signals to users the nature of⟦P39⟧changes in this release. For more, see ⟦P40⟧Module version⟦P41⟧numbering⟦P42⟧.⟦P43⟧Push the new tag to the origin repository.⟦P44⟧Make the module available by running the ⟦P45⟧⟦P46⟧⟦P47⟧command⟦P48⟧ to prompt⟦P49⟧Go to update its index of modules with information about the module you're⟦P50⟧publishing.⟦P51⟧Precede the command with a statement to set the ⟦P52⟧ environment⟦P53⟧variable to a Go proxy. This will ensure that your request reaches the⟦P54⟧proxy.⟦P55⟧Developers interested in your module import a package from it and run the ⟦P56⟧⟦P57⟧ command⟦P58⟧ just as they would with any other module. They can run the ⟦P59⟧⟦P60⟧ command⟦P61⟧ for latest versions or they can specify a particular version, as⟦P62⟧in the following example:⟦P63⟧
